## 2. The finite strip theorem

Use the paper normalization: minimum center distance two and triangular lattice

\[
\Lambda_2=\{(2i+j,\sqrt3j):i,j\in\mathbb Z\}.
\]

For `d>0`, the points with `j>=0` are shifted upward by `(0,d)`; the points with
`j<0` stay fixed. Denote this infinite, unit-separated-after-rescaling gap packing by
`P(d)`.

**Theorem 1 (finite alternating-strip widening).** Let `n>=3`, `r>=1`,
`0<d<=1/8`, and `rd<=2`. There is a finite three-stage, contact-feasible motion of
`4r+1` rigid triangular blocks of `P(d)`, with every other center fixed, such that:

1. the middle upward triangle of size `n-1` is translated exactly vertically;
2. its base gap increases from `d` to `d+v`;
3. the exact gain is

   \[
   \boxed{v={r^2d^4\over128\sqrt3};}
   \]

4. the support lies in a lattice trapezoid of height `n-1` and base-index width
   `(2r+1)(n+1)-3`;
5. all points, including points outside the blocks, remain at pairwise distance at
   least two throughout the motion.

In particular, choosing `r=ceil(1/d)` is admissible and yields

\[
\boxed{v\ge{d^2\over128\sqrt3}.}
\tag{1}
\]

This is a finite motion of the actual gap packing. It does not add points until the
terminal insertion is performed, and it does not assert that `P(d)` is globally
optimal.

### 2.1 Exact block partition

Put `a=n-1`. Tile the lattice trapezoid by upward triangles `U_0,...,U_{2r}` and
downward triangles `V_0,...,V_{2r-1}`. There are `4r+1` blocks in left-to-right order.
All upward triangles have size `n` except `U_r`, which has size `n-1`.

An explicit index definition removes any picture-dependent convention. Start with
`b_0=0`. For `k=0,...,2r`, put

\[
t_k=a-\mathbf1_{k=r},\qquad b_{k+1}=b_k+t_k+2.
\]

Then

\[
U_k=\{(i,j):0\le j\le t_k,\ b_k\le i\le b_k+t_k-j\},
\]

and, for `k<2r`,

\[
V_k=\{(i,j):0\le j\le a,\ b_k+t_k+1-j\le i\le b_k+t_k+1\}.
\]

The union is exactly

\[
0\le j\le n-1,\qquad
0\le i\le(2r+1)(n+1)-3-j.
\tag{2}
\]

The middle block is the upward triangle `U_r`. Label the blocks `1,...,4r+1` in
order, with middle label `2r+1`. Reflection in the vertical symmetry axis sends label
`ell` to `4r+2-ell`. At `r=1` this is exactly the canonical five-block partition.

### 2.2 Explicit final translations

Put

\[
\varepsilon={d^2\over16},\qquad
A_\ell=\ell\varepsilon\quad(1\le\ell\le2r),\qquad
c=A_{2r}={rd^2\over8},\qquad
v={c^2\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{3}
\]

For a block on the left of the middle, translate by

\[
\tau_\ell=
\begin{cases}
(-A_\ell,-d+A_\ell/\sqrt3),&\ell\text{ odd},\\
(-A_\ell,-A_\ell/\sqrt3),&\ell\text{ even}.
\end{cases}
\tag{4}
\]

Reflect these translations on the right. Translate the middle block by `(0,v)`.
Since `rd<=2`,

\[
0<A_\ell\le c\le d/4.
\tag{5}
\]

Thus upward blocks descend by `b_ell=d-A_ell/sqrt(3)>0`, downward blocks descend
by `A_ell/sqrt(3)`, and the horizontal translations accumulate toward the middle.

### 2.3 Complete interface audit

Within each block, distances are unchanged. Every cross-block original nearest
neighbor has one of the following types, up to reflection and reversing endpoints.
The table is exhaustive directly from the index partition (2). The two diagonal
directions to a fixed shore differ only by the sign of their horizontal unit entry;
the table lists the smaller squared-distance margin.

| Interface | Squared-distance margin above four |
| --- | --- |
| Upward block to fixed lower diagonal | `4A^2/3` |
| Downward block to fixed upper diagonal | `4A^2/3` |
| Upward block to fixed upper diagonal, or downward block to fixed lower diagonal | `2sqrt(3)d-4A+A^2+(d-A/sqrt(3))^2` |
| Outer upward block to fixed upper horizontal neighbor | `(2-epsilon)^2+(d-epsilon/sqrt(3))^2-4` |
| Consecutive nonmiddle blocks, horizontal | `(2-epsilon)^2+g^2-4`, with `g=d-(A+A')/sqrt(3)` |
| Consecutive nonmiddle blocks, diagonal | `(1-epsilon)^2+(sqrt(3)+g)^2-4` |
| Middle block to adjacent downward block, horizontal | `(2+c)^2+(c/sqrt(3)+v)^2-4` |
| Middle block to adjacent downward block, diagonal | `(1+c)^2+(sqrt(3)-c/sqrt(3)-v)^2-4` |
| Two downward blocks above the shortened middle triangle | `(2+2c)^2-4` |
| Middle block to fixed lower diagonal | `2sqrt(3)(d+v)+(d+v)^2` |

There is no middle-to-fixed-upper nearest neighbor: the middle triangle is shorter
by one row. There are no other moved-to-fixed horizontal interfaces: only the two
outer triangles meet the sides of the trapezoid.

All margins are nonnegative. For the adverse fixed diagonal margin, (5) gives

\[
2\sqrt3d-4A\ge(2\sqrt3-1)d>0.
\]

For consecutive nonmiddle blocks,

\[
g\ge d\left(1-{1\over2\sqrt3}\right)>{2d\over3}.
\]

Hence the horizontal margin is at least

\[
-4\varepsilon+g^2
\ge-{d^2\over4}+{4d^2\over9}={7d^2\over36}>0.
\tag{6}
\]

The outer horizontal margin has the same lower bound. The nonmiddle diagonal
margin is at least `-2epsilon+2sqrt(3)g>0`.

The only potentially restrictive middle margin simplifies exactly, using (3), to

\[
{4c^2\over3}-2(\sqrt3-c/\sqrt3)v+v^2
={c^2\over3}+{c^3\over3}+{c^4\over12}>0.
\tag{7}
\]

The remaining listed margins are manifestly nonnegative.

### 2.4 Finite integration, not merely an endpoint

The following three-stage path integrates the construction.

**Stage I.** Move every nonmiddle upward block vertically to its final height,
keeping all horizontal coordinates, all downward blocks, and the middle fixed.
Parameterize this stage by `s in [0,1]` and use vertical displacement
`-s(d-A/sqrt(3))`. Horizontal interface lengths increase; the diagonal interfaces
between upward and downward blocks open. Fixed-upper diagonal distances increase.
The remaining lower gap is at least `A/sqrt(3)>0`, so fixed-lower distances do not
fall below two.

**Stage II.** Keep those upward-block heights fixed. Set every left horizontal
translation to `-sA`, and every left downward-block vertical translation to
`-sA/sqrt(3)`, reflecting on the right. Keep the middle fixed. The upward-to-lower
worst margin is now

\[
(1-sA)^2+(\sqrt3+A/\sqrt3)^2-4
=2A(1-s)+s^2A^2+A^2/3\ge0.
\]

The downward-to-upper worst margin is `4s^2A^2/3`. The adverse fixed diagonal
margins are at least `2sqrt(3)d-4A>0`. Across each nonmiddle horizontal or
diagonal interface the vertical clearance is

\[
g_s=d-(A+sA')/\sqrt3\quad\text{or}\quad d-(sA+A')/\sqrt3,
\]

and therefore remains above `2d/3`. The horizontal increment is `s epsilon`, so
(6) remains a lower bound. The outer horizontal margin is also bounded below by
(6). Middle diagonal margins are `4s^2c^2/3`; middle horizontal and the two
downward-block horizontal margins increase.

**Stage III.** Move only the middle vertically from zero to `v`. Its lower and
horizontal distances increase. Its adverse diagonal margin decreases monotonically
until the endpoint (7), because `sqrt(3)-c/sqrt(3)-sv>0` throughout. Thus (7)
certifies the entire last stage.

At all stages every center displacement has norm below `2d`. Original
non-nearest-neighbor distances in `P(d)` are at least `2sqrt(3)`; opening the initial
gap cannot decrease a cross-gap distance. Such pairs therefore remain at distance
at least

\[
2\sqrt3-4d\ge2\sqrt3-1/2>2.
\]

This handles every non-neighbor pair, including pairs with a fixed endpoint. The
nearest-neighbor audit and the three-stage verification prove Theorem 1. ∎

## 3. Improved free aligned-gap support bound

**Theorem 2 (candidate support rate).** In the canonical minimum-center-distance-one
normalization, constants `C_1,C_2,C_3,d_0>0` exist such that

\[
\boxed{
W(d)\le C_1\exp\left({C_2\over d}\log{C_3\over d}\right)
\qquad(0<d<d_0).
}
\tag{8}
\]

Here `W` has exactly Section 150/157's free aligned-gap meaning. The exterior is
the complete gap packing, not an arbitrary source packing. The conclusion is
strictly stronger in exponent scale than the canonical `exp(O(d^-3))` bound.

### Proof

First work at center distance two. At current gap width `x<=1/8`, choose
`r(x)=ceil(1/x)` and apply Theorem 1. The new width is `x+v(x)`, where

\[
\kappa x^2\le v(x)\le Kx^2,
\qquad
\kappa={1\over128\sqrt3},
\quad K={(9/8)^2\over128\sqrt3}.
\tag{9}
\]

It takes at most `C/x_initial` stages to reach `1/8`: while below that threshold,

\[
{1\over x}-{1\over x+v(x)}
={v(x)\over x(x+v(x))}
\ge{\kappa\over1+K/8}>0.
\]

Once width `1/8` is reached, the canonical finite-gap insertion theorem provides a
fixed finite positive-cardinality witness. Its support and radius-three interaction collar
have fixed size, independent of the initial width. Choose the last middle triangle
large enough to contain that witness's upper-half support and collar. Its lower-half
support lies in the unchanged lower half-lattice. A wider terminal gap causes no
problem: the last middle lift may be reduced to give exactly width `1/8`, since
Stage III remains feasible for every smaller positive lift.

The preceding widening templates can be nested backwards into successive middle
triangles. Formula (2) gives base-index width `(2r+1)(n+1)-3`. A fixed lattice collar
around its upper-half footprint fits into a preceding upward triangle whenever the
preceding size is at least

\[
(2r+1)(n+1)+C_0.
\tag{10}
\]

The collar is necessary: every nearest neighbor of a newly moved upper point must
still be in the uniformly translated preceding middle triangle. Only the original
lower half-lattice is allowed across the base. A fixed extra lattice-index buffer
on the sloping sides and top achieves this, with `C_0` independent of `r,n,x`.

At width `x>=d`, the nesting factor in (10) is at most `C_4/d`. There are at most
`C_5/d` stages. Thus the initial size and the full support radius are bounded by

\[
C_6(C_4/d)^{C_5/d}
=C_6\exp\left({C_5\over d}\log{C_4\over d}\right).
\tag{11}
\]

The collar also proves nearest-neighbor compatibility between successive templates.
For non-neighbors, all ancestral middle shifts telescope into the difference of
current and initial gap widths. Before the terminal insertion, each original point's
total displacement is below `3/8`: accumulated middle lifts are at most `1/8`, and
its own nonmiddle translation has norm below `2(1/8)`. Therefore an original
non-neighbor pair remains at distance above `2sqrt(3)-3/4>2`. At the terminal step,
the complete finite witness and its radius-three interaction collar lie in the
final uniform gap region. All original upper centers in that collar are therefore
the exact centers of the canonical terminal gap packing. Every upper center outside
the collar differs from its hypothetical position in the complete final gap packing
by less than `1/2`: its displacement from the original initial-gap packing is below
`3/8`, and the two uniform gap heights differ by at most `1/8`. Thus an outside
center remains at distance above `3-1/2>2` from every changed or new terminal
point. Lower centers are exactly those of the complete terminal gap packing. The
canonical exterior-compatibility theorem therefore applies to the replacement,
including actual exterior points beyond the last triangle.

The insertion gains at least one center. Rescaling all lengths by one half changes
the constants in (11), and proves (8). ∎

## 4. Push to the same-hull capacity consequence

**Corollary 3 (candidate vanishing-strain range).** Retain Section 148's fixed
full-dimensional convex triangular-lattice polygon `K`, with `0 in int K`, and let
`P_L=Lambda cap LK`. Suppose `alpha_L>0`, `alpha_L->0`, and

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_L^{-1}\log(1/\alpha_L)=o(\log L).
}
\tag{12}
\]

For all sufficiently large integer `L`, the hull `(L+alpha_L)K` contains a
unit-separated set with `|P_L|+1` points.

### Proof

Choose `rho>0` with `rho B subset K`. Split `P_L` along a primitive horizontal
lattice row and translate the two halves by `+/- a_L` in the normal direction,
where `a_L=rho alpha_L/2`. Exactly as in Section 148, the split patch stays in
`(L+alpha_L)K`, has the same cardinality, and creates a free aligned gap of width
`rho alpha_L`. By (8) and (12), the insertion witness and its collar have radius

\[
\exp\{O(\alpha_L^{-1}\log(1/\alpha_L))\}=L^{o(1)}=o(L).
\]

They therefore fit in the fixed-shape expanding patch and hull. Replace the old
witness by the new one and retain exactly one extra point. ∎

For example, the sufficient regime includes
`alpha_L=(log log L)^2/log L`, which is far below the old sufficient scale
`(log L)^(-1/3)`. More generally it includes any
`alpha_L=(log log L)^(1+eta)/log L` for fixed `eta>0`.

This consequence reaches the exact same-hull cardinality endpoint for that model.
It does **not** create an arbitrary-source Schur kernel. An embedded source patch
must separately permit the row split and contain the complete moving/insertion
footprint without conflicts with exterior source points.

## 5. Failed first attack and repaired mechanism

The first test allowed rigid rotations of the canonical five blocks, followed by
independent motion of each original point in the same footprint. Floating-point
optimization at several small widths returned the canonical toe-out gain, with no
useful rotational improvement. That is discovery evidence, not an optimality
certificate; no theorem rejecting rotations or arbitrary internal deformation is
claimed.

The repaired attack changes the footprint. It concatenates many alternating upward
and downward triangles along the existing gap. Horizontal clearance of order `d^2`
at each interface accumulates into an inner translation of order `rd^2`; the middle
quadratic cage then permits gain of order `r^2d^4`. Taking `r=ceil(1/d)` turns this
into a quadratic gain. Equations (3)–(7) are an exact construction, not a numerical
survival claim.

The stronger proposed shortcut “every finite local rigid-block gap move has uniformly
quartic gain regardless of its number of blocks” is therefore false. Theorem 1
provides an exact family disproving it. Sections 161–162 never made that shortcut.

